title: Capital One 2019
page: breaches/capital-one.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: A former AWS engineer used SSRF via a misconfigured WAF to steal IAM role credentials; an over-privileged role then granted access to 700+ S3 buckets holding 106 million customer records, and the attack ran undetected for 117 days.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 106M | records exposed | 100M US and 6M Canadian credit card application records
stat: 117 | days of dwell time | undetected until a GitHub user reported it to Capital One
stat: $80M | civil penalty | the page gives no date or issuer for it
Threat actor, per the page: Paige Thompson, a former AWS employee. The page says the attack directly caused AWS to build IMDSv2.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with.

The page counts 106 million customer records exposed, and breaks them down as 100 million US and 6 million Canadian credit card application records.

The attack ran undetected for 117 days. Dwell time is how long an attacker is in an environment before detection. Here no internal monitoring caught the breach; a GitHub user who saw Thompson's post reported it to Capital One.

The page lists an $80M civil penalty. It does not say who imposed it or when, so do not add either.

The page names the threat actor as Paige Thompson, a former AWS employee, and its summary describes a former AWS engineer. It also says the attack directly caused AWS to build IMDSv2, the second version of the EC2 instance metadata service, which later slides explain.

Capital One was not the only alleged target. It says the superseding indictment alleges Thompson also took data from other organizations, among them a US state agency, a telecommunications conglomerate outside the US, a public research university and four technology companies. Keep the word alleges: these are the indictment's allegations.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: March to July 2019 | The incident window the page gives
event: 117 days | The attack runs undetected
event: July 17, 2019 | A GitHub user who saw Thompson's post emails Capital One's responsible disclosure address
event: July 29, 2019 | Paige Thompson is arrested
event: Date not given | AWS builds IMDSv2, which the page says the attack directly caused
The page does not date the scanning, the SSRF or the sync, and gives no start day. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives only a few dates, and the timeline shows only those.

The page dates the incident to March through July 2019. The attack ran undetected for 117 days, and no internal monitoring caught it during that dwell period. The page gives no start day, so do not count back from July 17 to name one.

On July 17, 2019, a GitHub user who saw Thompson's post emailed Capital One's responsible disclosure address. The Justice Department says the user "alerted Capital One to the possibility it had suffered a data theft." Paige Thompson was arrested on July 29, 2019.

The page says the attack directly caused AWS to build IMDSv2, and gives no date for that.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Reconnaissance | Scanning for SSRF-vulnerable apps | T1595
step: 02 | Initial Access | SSRF requests reach IMDS | T1190
step: 03 | Credential Access | Role credentials from IMDSv1 | T1552.005
step: 04 | Privilege Abuse | 700+ S3 buckets listed | T1619
step: 05 | Exfiltration | About 30GB synced out | T1530
step: 06 | Discovery | Reported 117 days later | -
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stage 06 is how the breach came to light, and carries no ATT&CK technique.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Reconnaissance, is automated scanning for AWS-hosted applications vulnerable to SSRF. Stage 02, Initial Access, is the SSRF itself: a firewall misconfiguration let crafted requests reach the instance metadata service, and the page's sources differ on how. Stage 03, Credential Access, is IMDSv1 returning the role's temporary credentials. Stage 04 sits under the page's Privilege Abuse phase: the stolen credentials listed 700+ S3 buckets. Stage 05, Exfiltration, is approximately 30GB synced to external storage. Stage 06, which the page labels Discovery, is how the breach came to light 117 days later, and it carries no ATT&CK technique.

The page's own title runs the chain the same way: SSRF, then IMDSv1, then an over-privileged IAM role, then 106 million records taken from S3. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A custom tool scanned for apps that would relay metadata requests
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Reconnaissance
technique: T1595 | Active Scanning
fact: Scanned for | EC2-hosted web apps that would relay requests to 169.254.169.254
fact: Target | a public-facing ModSecurity WAF running on EC2
fact: Approach | automated, targeting multiple AWS-hosted organisations
SSRF was not in ModSecurity's default detection rule set and had to be explicitly configured.
notes:
Stage 01 is reconnaissance. The page maps it to T1595, Active Scanning.

Thompson built a custom tool to scan the internet for EC2-hosted web applications that would relay requests to the AWS instance metadata service at 169.254.169.254. EC2 is AWS's virtual machine service, and the instance metadata service is an endpoint an instance queries for information about itself. The page says the scanning was automated and targeted multiple AWS-hosted organisations. It names none of the others, so do not name any.

The target here was a public-facing ModSecurity web application firewall, a WAF, running on EC2.

Why was SSRF not blocked? The page's answer is that SSRF was not in ModSecurity's default detection rule set and had to be explicitly configured. SSRF is server-side request forgery. The next slide explains WAFs in general terms, and a background slide after stage 02 explains SSRF.

== What a WAF is, and what ModSecurity is
layout: background
source: OWASP, Web Application Firewall | https://community.owasp.org/Web_Application_Firewall
credit: Also from OWASP ModSecurity (owasp.org)
A web application firewall applies a set of rules to an HTTP conversation; the rules generally cover common attacks like cross-site scripting and SQL injection. ModSecurity is an open-source WAF engine.
notes:
Background, from OWASP, in general terms.

OWASP describes a web application firewall as an application firewall for HTTP applications that applies a set of rules to an HTTP conversation. Generally those rules cover common attacks such as cross-site scripting and SQL injection. Where proxies generally protect clients, OWASP says, WAFs protect servers. A WAF is deployed to protect a specific web application or set of web applications.

A WAF may come as an appliance, a server plugin or a filter, and may be customized to an application. OWASP says the effort to perform this customization can be significant, and needs to be maintained as the application is modified.

OWASP describes ModSecurity as an open-source web application firewall engine, originally designed as a module for the Apache HTTP Server. It now provides HTTP request and response filtering on several platforms, including Apache HTTP Server, Microsoft IIS and Nginx.

Also from OWASP ModSecurity: https://owasp.org/projects/modsecurity

== A firewall misconfiguration let crafted requests reach IMDS
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: Target URL | /latest/meta-data/ on 169.254.169.254
fact: Effect | the EC2 instance itself issued the metadata request
fact: IMDSv1 behaviour | no authentication; any GET request from the instance is served
fact: WAF failure | did not block the SSRF payload; sources differ on how
The ACM analysis calls this step "the source of some controversy and speculation."
notes:
Stage 02 is initial access. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application.

Thompson sent crafted HTTP requests containing the IMDS endpoint, http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/, as the target URL, making the EC2 instance itself issue the metadata request. IMDS is the instance metadata service.

That matters because of how IMDSv1, the original version of the service, behaved: no authentication, and any GET request from the instance is served.

How the requests got past the firewall is less certain. The page says the WAF did not block the SSRF payload, and reports differing accounts of how, each attributed. The ACM analysis calls this step "the source of some controversy and speculation."

The next slide sets the accounts side by side, and the one after explains server-side request forgery in general terms.

== What each source says happened at the firewall
layout: compare
col: The DOJ complaint says only | "A firewall misconfiguration permitted commands to reach and be executed by" the server
col: Krebs's source, and ACM | The misconfigured WAF was tricked into relaying requests to the metadata service
col: Appsecco suggests instead | An SSRF in an application behind the WAF | Either a bypass was used, or the WAF was in logging mode
The ACM analysis calls this step "the source of some controversy and speculation."
notes:
The page gives three accounts of how the crafted requests got through, and attributes each one.

The Justice Department's complaint says only that "a firewall misconfiguration permitted commands to reach and be executed by" the server.

Krebs's source says the misconfigured WAF was tricked into relaying requests to the metadata service, and the page says that is the position the ACM analysis takes.

Appsecco suggests instead an SSRF in an application behind the WAF, with either a bypass used or the WAF not configured to block attacks, which it calls logging mode.

The ACM analysis calls this step "the source of some controversy and speculation." Keep each account with its source when you tell it, and do not present any one of them as settled.

== What server-side request forgery is
layout: background
source: OWASP Server-Side Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, SSRF (csoh.org)
SSRF abuses an application to interact with the internal or external network, or the machine itself. OWASP says that in cloud environments, SSRF is often used to steal credentials and access tokens from metadata services.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's Server-Side Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet and the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

OWASP describes SSRF as an attack vector that abuses an application to interact with the internal or external network, or with the machine itself. One enabler it names is the mishandling of URLs: for example, a user supplying an image URL for the application to download, a custom webhook or callback URL, or internal requests to another service. The csoh.org glossary puts it more simply: trick a server into fetching an attacker-chosen URL.

OWASP adds that SSRF is not limited to HTTP. The first request is generally HTTP, but a second request the application makes can use other protocols and schemes.

In cloud environments, OWASP says, SSRF is often used to access and steal credentials and access tokens from metadata services, such as the AWS Instance Metadata Service, the Azure Instance Metadata Service and the GCP metadata server. The glossary calls SSRF the historical path to IMDS credentials in cloud.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-ssrf

== IMDSv1 returned the role's temporary credentials, no auth required
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.005 | Unsecured Credentials: Cloud Instance Metadata API
fact: Role | ISRM-WAF-Role, attached to the EC2 instance
fact: Returned | AccessKeyId, SecretAccessKey and SessionToken
fact: Required | no token, header or authentication
fact: Key problem | the role had S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs
All IMDSv1 required was a GET from within the instance, which the SSRF provided.
notes:
Stage 03 is credential access. The page maps it to T1552.005, Cloud Instance Metadata API, under Unsecured Credentials.

IMDSv1 returned the temporary AWS credentials for the ISRM-WAF-Role, the IAM role attached to the EC2 instance: an AccessKeyId, a SecretAccessKey and a SessionToken. IAM is AWS's identity and access management. The metadata path was /latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ISRM-WAF-Role.

No token, header or authentication was required, just a GET request to the metadata path from within the instance, which the SSRF provided.

The page already names the key problem at this stage: the role had S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs. Stage 04 shows what those permissions allowed.

The next slide explains IMDSv1 and IMDSv2 in general terms.

== What IMDSv1 and IMDSv2 do
layout: background
source: AWS EC2 documentation, Use the Instance Metadata Service | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/configuring-instance-metadata-service.html
credit: Also from Retrieve security credentials from instance metadata, and IAM roles for Amazon EC2 (docs.aws.amazon.com)
An application on an EC2 instance retrieves its IAM role's temporary credentials from instance metadata. IMDSv1 is a plain request and response. IMDSv2 first needs a PUT request, which returns a token each GET must carry.
notes:
Background, from AWS's EC2 documentation, in general terms.

AWS designed IAM roles for EC2 so that applications on an instance can make AWS API requests without you having to manage the security credentials they use. An application retrieves the role's security credentials from the instance metadata item iam/security-credentials/ followed by the role name. AWS says these credentials are temporary and that it rotates them automatically.

IMDSv1 is a request and response method. IMDSv2 is session-oriented: a PUT request returns a session token, and the token must be included in subsequent GET requests. The token is instance-specific, and is rejected if used outside the instance on which it was generated. AWS rejects a token PUT that carries an X-Forwarded-For header, and by default the PUT response has a hop limit of 1.

An instance can be configured to accept only IMDSv2 calls, which causes IMDSv1 calls to fail. For an extensive review of IMDSv2, AWS's documentation points to its post on defense in depth against open firewalls, reverse proxies and SSRF vulnerabilities.

Also from Retrieve security credentials from instance metadata: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/instance-metadata-security-credentials.html

And from IAM roles for Amazon EC2: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/iam-roles-for-amazon-ec2.html

== Stolen role credentials listed 700+ S3 buckets
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Privilege Abuse: Over-Permissioned IAM Role
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Command | aws s3 ls, with the stolen session credentials
fact: Result | 700+ buckets, including Capital One customer data stores
fact: Root failure | permissions never reviewed against least privilege
The role's sweeping S3 list and read permissions went far beyond what a WAF firewall function ever needed.
notes:
Stage 04 sits under the page's phase Privilege Abuse: Over-Permissioned IAM Role. The page maps it to T1619, Cloud Storage Object Discovery.

With the stolen credentials, Thompson used the CLI, the AWS command line interface, to list all S3 buckets accessible to the ISRM-WAF-Role. The command was aws s3 ls, authenticated with the stolen session credentials. It listed 700+ buckets, including Capital One customer data stores. S3 is AWS's object storage service.

The page puts the root failure in the role's design. The role had been granted sweeping S3 list and read permissions, far beyond what a WAF firewall function ever needed, in violation of least privilege at the design level. Least privilege means granting only the permissions a task needs. The page says the role's permissions were never reviewed against that principle.

== About 30GB of S3 data synced to external storage
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1530 | Data from Cloud Storage
fact: Tool | aws s3 sync, to external storage
fact: Volume | approximately 30GB, over multiple sessions
fact: Records | 106M customer records
fact: Detection gap | GuardDuty not enabled
S3 access logs were not monitored for volume anomalies.
notes:
Stage 05 is exfiltration. The page maps it to T1530, Data from Cloud Storage.

Thompson synced S3 bucket contents to external storage using aws s3 sync: approximately 30GB, over multiple sessions. The page lists what it held: 100 million US and 6 million Canadian credit card application records, 140,000 SSNs, 80,000 bank account numbers, credit scores, and fragments of transaction data from a total of 23 days in 2016, 2017 and 2018. Its step code also lists payment history. SSN stands for Social Security number. The next slide sets those figures side by side.

The page names the detection gap at this stage. GuardDuty, AWS's threat detection service, was not enabled, and S3 access logs were not monitored for volume anomalies. Both come back in the defenses.

== What the roughly 30GB held
layout: stats
stat: 100M | US records | credit card application records
stat: 6M | Canadian records | credit card application records
stat: 140K | SSNs | Social Security numbers
stat: 80K | bank account numbers | alongside credit scores and payment history
Approximately 30GB, over multiple sessions, with fragments of transaction data from a total of 23 days in 2016, 2017 and 2018.
notes:
These are the figures the page gives for what was taken at stage 05.

Approximately 30GB was synced over multiple sessions. It held 100 million US and 6 million Canadian credit card application records, which together match the 106 million records in the page's headline. It also held 140,000 Social Security numbers and 80,000 bank account numbers, along with credit scores, payment history, and fragments of transaction data from a total of 23 days in 2016, 2017 and 2018.

The page counts records, not people, so keep its unit, and do not break the counts down further.

== Reported 117 days later, after Thompson bragged on GitHub, Slack and Twitter
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Discovery
fact: Bragging | on GitHub, and on Slack and Twitter under the handle "erratic"
fact: Reported by | a GitHub user who saw the post
fact: Reported on | July 17, 2019, to Capital One's responsible disclosure address
fact: Monitoring failures | no GuardDuty, no S3 volume alerts, no anomalous IAM activity detection
No internal monitoring caught the breach during the 117-day dwell period.
notes:
Stage 06 is how the breach came to light; the page labels the phase Discovery, and maps the stage to no ATT&CK technique.

Thompson bragged about the breach on GitHub, and on Slack and Twitter under the handle "erratic". On July 17, 2019, a GitHub user who saw the post emailed Capital One's responsible disclosure address. The Justice Department says the user "alerted Capital One to the possibility it had suffered a data theft." The page calls this an external tipster, through Capital One's responsible disclosure program. Paige Thompson was arrested on July 29, 2019.

No internal monitoring caught the breach during the 117-day dwell period: not GuardDuty, not S3 access logs, not IAM anomaly detection.

Two of the page's defenses, GuardDuty and alerts on S3 access volume, answer this stage.

== The failure the page names at each stage
layout: table
row: Stage | The failure the page names
row: 01 | SSRF not in default WAF rules; required manual configuration
row: 02 | The WAF did not block the SSRF payload; sources differ on how
row: 03 | The role had S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs
row: 04 | Role permissions never reviewed against least privilege
row: 05 | GuardDuty not enabled; S3 access logs not monitored for volume anomalies
row: 06 | No GuardDuty, no S3 volume alerts, no anomalous IAM activity detection
notes:
The page labels a failure at every stage, and this table restates each one.

At stage 01, SSRF was not blocked because it was not in the default WAF rules and required manual configuration. At stage 02, the WAF did not block the SSRF payload: it relayed it, per Krebs's source and ACM, or was bypassed or only logging, per Appsecco. At stage 03, the page calls the key problem a role with S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs. At stage 04, the root failure is that the IAM role's permissions were never reviewed against the principle of least privilege. At stage 05, the detection gap is GuardDuty not enabled and S3 access logs not monitored for volume anomalies. At stage 06, the monitoring failures are no GuardDuty, no S3 volume alerts and no anomalous IAM activity detection.

The next slide sets the page's five defenses against the six stages.

== Five defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Enforce IMDSv2 on all EC2 instances | 03
row: Least-privilege IAM on every role | 03 | 04
row: Enable Amazon GuardDuty | 05 | 06
row: Alert on anomalous S3 access volume | 05 | 06
row: Add SSRF rules to your WAF | 01 | 02
Every stage has at least one defense the page ties to it.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's five defenses against its six stages. A cell is marked only where the page's own words tie that defense to that stage.

Enforcing IMDSv2 answers stage 03, where IMDSv1 returned the role's credentials to a GET request with no token. Least-privilege IAM answers stages 03 and 04. Stage 03 names as its key problem a role with S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs, and at stage 04 those sweeping S3 list and read permissions let 700+ buckets be listed.

GuardDuty and alerts on S3 access volume answer stages 05 and 06. Stage 05 names GuardDuty not enabled and S3 access logs not monitored for volume anomalies as its detection gap, and stage 06 lists no GuardDuty and no S3 volume alerts among its monitoring failures.

Adding SSRF rules to the WAF answers stages 01 and 02. At stage 01 the page says SSRF was not in ModSecurity's default rule set and had to be explicitly configured, and the defense says the same of default WAF configurations. At stage 02 the WAF did not block the SSRF payload aimed at 169.254.169.254, though the page's sources differ on how.

Every column has at least one mark.

== Enforce IMDSv2, and give every role least privilege
layout: compare
col: Enforce IMDSv2 (stage 03) | IMDSv2 needs a PUT for a session token, so GET-only SSRF cannot obtain credentials | AWS's documentation says "By default, you can use either IMDSv1 or IMDSv2, or both." | Set the account-level default to IMDSv2; enforce it via SCP
col: Least privilege (stages 03, 04) | A WAF role should only write WAF logs, not list or read S3 | Use IAM Access Analyzer findings and Access Advisor unused-permission reports | Identify and tighten over-privileged roles
notes:
The page's first two defenses answer stages 03 and 04.

Enforce IMDSv2 on all EC2 instances. IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained via a PUT request, so SSRF attacks that can only issue GET requests cannot obtain credentials. That answers stage 03, where IMDSv1 handed the role's credentials to a GET request with no token. AWS's documentation says "By default, you can use either IMDSv1 or IMDSv2, or both." The page says console Quick Start launches and Amazon Linux 2023 default to IMDSv2 only, and that AWS said instance types released from mid-2024 would too, but other launches still accept IMDSv1 unless you change that. So set the account-level default to IMDSv2, and enforce it across your organisation with an SCP, a service control policy in AWS Organizations.

Apply least-privilege IAM to every role. The page says a WAF role should only write WAF logs, not list or read S3. That answers stage 03's key problem, a role with S3 permissions far beyond what a WAF needs, and stage 04, where those sweeping S3 list and read permissions let 700+ buckets be listed. Use IAM Access Analyzer findings and Access Advisor unused-permission reports to identify and tighten over-privileged roles. IAM Access Analyzer is an AWS service that, among other things, surfaces unused permissions.

== Enable GuardDuty, and alert on S3 access volume
layout: compare
col: Enable Amazon GuardDuty (stages 05, 06) | Its .OutsideAWS finding detects EC2 instance credentials used from an external IP address | Its .InsideAWS counterpart detects their use from another AWS account
col: Alert on S3 access volume (stages 05, 06) | Large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls | From an unexpected IP or at an unusual rate | Should trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert immediately
No internal monitoring caught the breach during the 117-day dwell period.
notes:
The page's next two defenses are detection, and both answer stages 05 and 06, where the page lists GuardDuty and S3 volume alerts among what was missing.

Enable Amazon GuardDuty. The page says its UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS finding detects credentials created for an EC2 instance being used from an external IP address, and its .InsideAWS counterpart detects their use from another AWS account. At stage 05 GuardDuty was not enabled, and stage 06 lists no GuardDuty among its monitoring failures.

Alert on anomalous S3 access volume. Large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls from an unexpected IP or at an unusual rate should trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert immediately. At stage 05, S3 access logs were not monitored for volume anomalies, and at stage 06 the page lists no S3 volume alerts. CloudWatch is AWS's monitoring service, and SNS is its Simple Notification Service.

The page does not say what either control would have caught in this incident, so do not claim either would have shortened the 117 days.

== Add SSRF rules to your WAF
layout: text
- Block requests containing 169.254.169.254 in URL, body, or headers.
- This is not in default WAF configurations; it must be explicitly added.
- The rule answers stages 01 and 02.
notes:
The last defense answers stages 01 and 02.

Add SSRF rules to your WAF: block requests containing 169.254.169.254 in the URL, body or headers. The page says this is not in default WAF configurations, and must be explicitly added.

That matches what stage 01 found. SSRF was not in ModSecurity's default detection rule set and had to be explicitly configured. At stage 02, the WAF did not block the SSRF payload aimed at 169.254.169.254. The page reports differing accounts of how: relayed, per Krebs's source and ACM, or bypassed or only logging, per Appsecco.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Do all your EC2 instances require IMDSv2, or do some still accept IMDSv1?
check: Is your account-level default IMDSv2, with an SCP enforcing it organisation-wide?
check: Does any role hold S3 list or read permissions its job does not need?
check: Do you act on IAM Access Analyzer findings and Access Advisor unused-permission reports?
check: Is Amazon GuardDuty enabled, so its instance credential exfiltration findings can fire?
check: Would large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert?
check: Does your WAF block requests containing 169.254.169.254 in URL, body or headers?
notes:
Turn the page's five defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them; the first defense gives two.

Ask whether every EC2 instance requires IMDSv2, or whether some still accept IMDSv1, since AWS's documentation says that by default you can use either IMDSv1 or IMDSv2, or both. Ask whether the account-level default is IMDSv2, and whether an SCP enforces it across the organisation. Ask whether any role, a WAF's or any other, holds S3 list or read permissions its job does not need, and whether anyone acts on IAM Access Analyzer findings and Access Advisor unused-permission reports.

Ask whether Amazon GuardDuty is enabled, so its findings for EC2 instance credentials used from an external IP address or from another AWS account can fire. Ask whether large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls from an unexpected IP, or at an unusual rate, would trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert.

Finally, ask whether your WAF blocks requests containing 169.254.169.254 in the URL, body or headers, since the page says that rule is not in default configurations.

== Which of your instances would hand credentials to a plain GET?
layout: question
IMDSv1 returned the ISRM-WAF-Role's temporary credentials to a GET request, with no token, header or authentication.

Name one instance or workload where you could not say for certain that IMDSv2 is required.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

At stage 03, IMDSv1 returned the role's temporary credentials, an AccessKeyId, a SecretAccessKey and a SessionToken, to a GET request from within the instance. No token, header or authentication was required, and the SSRF provided the request.

Ask each person to name one instance or workload before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says about IMDSv2.

== What the page says about IMDSv2
layout: answer
- IMDSv2 requires a PUT for a session token, so GET-only SSRF cannot obtain credentials.
- AWS's documentation says "By default, you can use either IMDSv1 or IMDSv2, or both."
- Set the account-level default to IMDSv2, and enforce it via SCP.
notes:
The page's first defense is to enforce IMDSv2 on all EC2 instances.

IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained via a PUT request, so SSRF attacks that can only issue GET requests cannot obtain credentials. AWS's documentation says "By default, you can use either IMDSv1 or IMDSv2, or both." The page says some launch paths default to IMDSv2 only, but other launches still accept IMDSv1 unless you change that, so set the account-level default to IMDSv2 and enforce it across your organisation with a service control policy.

Bring the group's examples back to this. An instance that still serves credentials to a GET request with no token is the condition stage 03 relied on. The page also says the attack directly caused AWS to build IMDSv2.

== If nobody had bragged, what would have told you?
layout: question
The breach was reported 117 days later, by a GitHub user who saw Thompson's post. No internal monitoring caught it.

What in your environment would have noticed stage 04 or stage 05?
notes:
This question comes from stage 06.

Thompson bragged about the breach on GitHub, Slack and Twitter. On July 17, 2019, a GitHub user who saw the post emailed Capital One's responsible disclosure address. No internal monitoring caught the breach during the 117-day dwell period: not GuardDuty, not S3 access logs, not IAM anomaly detection.

Stage 04 listed 700+ buckets with stolen role credentials. Stage 05 synced approximately 30GB to external storage over multiple sessions. Let the group name what in their own environment would notice either one, before you show the answer slide.

== The page's two detection defenses
layout: answer
- GuardDuty's .OutsideAWS and .InsideAWS findings detect EC2 instance credentials used from an external IP or another AWS account.
- Large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls from an unexpected IP or at an unusual rate should trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert.
notes:
The page gives two detection defenses, and both answer stages 05 and 06.

Enable Amazon GuardDuty. Its UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS finding detects credentials created for an EC2 instance being used from an external IP address, and its .InsideAWS counterpart detects their use from another AWS account.

Alert on anomalous S3 access volume. Large-scale GetObject or ListBucket calls from an unexpected IP or at an unusual rate should trigger a CloudWatch and SNS alert immediately.

The page lists both among what was missing: GuardDuty was not enabled, and S3 access logs were not monitored for volume anomalies. Test the group's answers against those two. The page does not say what either would have caught in this incident, so do not claim either would have shortened the 117 days.

