link: AWS IMDSv2 response | https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/defense-in-depth-open-firewalls-reverse-proxies-ssrf-vulnerabilities-ec2-instance-metadata-service/
link: ACM systematic analysis | https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3546068
link: MITRE ATT&CK T1552.005 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/005/
link: Superseding indictment, US v. Thompson (PDF) | https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdwa/page/file/1405446/dl?inline
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

KrebsOnSecurity's post-mortem and Appsecco's technical analysis, linked as an archived copy, are in the page's header, alongside AWS's IMDSv2 response, its post on defense in depth against open firewalls, reverse proxies and SSRF vulnerabilities. The page also cites a systematic analysis in the ACM Digital Library (ACM is the Association for Computing Machinery), which it quotes at stage 02, and the MITRE page that defines T1552.005, the technique behind stage 03. The superseding indictment in US v. Thompson, a Justice Department PDF, is the source for the other organizations Thompson allegedly took data from.

The background slides name their own sources: OWASP, the csoh.org glossary and AWS's EC2 documentation.

